Do not leave DELETING behind when a server delete fails

`API._delete` (nova/compute/api.py) saves task_state DELETING before it
decides how to delete. With the compute service reported down it runs
`_local_delete`, which calls Neutron, Cinder and Placement. When one of them
fails, the user gets a 409 or a 500 and the instance keeps DELETING. Once the
service reports again, `_delete` finds DELETING, logs "Instance is already in
deleting state, ignoring this request" and returns: every later DELETE
answers 204 and does nothing until nova-compute restarts, because no compute
service owns the delete. `_init_instance` (nova/compute/manager.py) has the
same gap: when the deletion it restarts at service start fails before
`_delete_instance` cleared the task state, it sets ERROR and keeps DELETING.

Nightly chaos found this on the random leg (issues #1575, #1581, #1582 and
#1583). A MariaDB outage is enough: nova-compute misses its service reports
while the database is down, so the API deletes locally on a healthy compute,
and the Neutron call fails in the same outage. A kill of nova-compute during
a delete that overlaps a partition between Placement and its database gets
there too. dizzy's teardown then timed out on the server and exited 1.

The failed local delete now clears the task state before the error goes out,
and `_init_instance` clears it when it sets ERROR. The next DELETE then
reaches the compute service, which finishes the job: `_delete_instance`
tolerates the ports and volumes the failed attempt already removed. The
compute side no longer retries such a server at its next start, which is
fine: a failed deletion surfaces as ERROR, and the user's DELETE works again.

Tests: test_init_instance_stuck_in_deleting_raises_exception pins the
`_set_instance_obj_error_state` call and moves with the patch. The new
test_delete_failed_local_delete_clears_task_state fails without it.

Proof: tests/container-images/verify_nova.sh asserts in the built image that
a local delete that fails with AllocationDeleteFailed leaves no task state.

Applies to nova 33.0.0 (2026.1).

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

diff --git a/nova/compute/api.py b/nova/compute/api.py
index d31769c..3432a37 100644
--- a/nova/compute/api.py
+++ b/nova/compute/api.py
@@ -2616,7 +2616,18 @@ class API:
                 LOG.debug('Doing local delete in cell %s', cell.identity,
                           instance=instance)
                 with nova_context.target_cell(context, cell) as cctxt:
-                    self._local_delete(cctxt, instance, bdms, delete_type, cb)
+                    try:
+                        self._local_delete(cctxt, instance, bdms, delete_type,
+                                           cb)
+                    except exception.InstanceNotFound:
+                        raise
+                    except Exception:
+                        # No compute service owns this delete, so a task
+                        # state of DELETING would make every later delete
+                        # request a no-op once the service is back up.
+                        with excutils.save_and_reraise_exception():
+                            instance.task_state = None
+                            instance.save()
                     self._record_action_start(context, instance,
                                               instance_actions.DELETE)
 
diff --git a/nova/compute/manager.py b/nova/compute/manager.py
index ce6b98d..399eb15 100644
--- a/nova/compute/manager.py
+++ b/nova/compute/manager.py
@@ -1282,7 +1282,11 @@ class ComputeManager(manager.Manager):
                 # we don't want that an exception blocks the init_host
                 LOG.exception('Failed to complete a deletion',
                               instance=instance)
-                self._set_instance_obj_error_state(instance)
+                # NOTE: Nothing retries the deletion until the next restart,
+                # and the API ignores a delete request while the task state
+                # is DELETING, so clear it.
+                self._set_instance_obj_error_state(instance,
+                                                   clean_task_state=True)
             return
 
         current_power_state = self._get_power_state(instance)
